Giant cell arteritis
Rheumatology & Immunology
New-onset temporal headache, Jaw claudication, Markedly elevated ESR

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-017
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe unilateral headache, extreme scalp tenderness preventing hair brushing, and jaw pain while eating, necessitating immediate evaluation to rule out imminent irreversible blindness.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a classic presentation of temporal arteritis with new-onset jaw claudication, a definitive red flag for severe cranial ischemia. Urgent after-hours bedside assessment was absolutely critical to physically palpate the temporal arteries, assess visual acuity, and initiate emergency high-dose systemic corticosteroids, as waiting until the next in-hours period posed an unacceptable, immediate risk of sudden, permanent vision loss.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My head is throbbing so badly on the right side I can't even touch my hair, and my jaw aches terribly when I try to chew my food."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 5-day history of a new-onset, severe, localized headache typically concentrated in the right temporal region. Over the past 48 hours, the pain has become relentless. Yesterday, the patient experienced the onset of jaw claudication, characterized by a deep aching pain and rapid fatigue in the masseter muscles after chewing for only a minute. Reports profound scalp tenderness that is noticeably aggravated when brushing hair or resting the head on a pillow. Systemic symptoms including low-grade fever, profound fatigue, and generalized malaise have been present for two weeks. Denies any sudden, painless vision loss (amaurosis fugax) or diplopia currently. Also notes proximal shoulder stiffness (Polymyalgia rheumatica symptoms) particularly in the mornings.
Functional Status: Unable to sleep or eat solid food due to severe localized pain; mobilizing safely but severely distressed.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of sudden blindness; adult child is highly anxious.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/85 mmHg | HR: 92 bpm | Temp: 37.8 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient appears acutely unwell, holding the right side of their head, avoiding any contact with the temporal area.
Physical Exam: HEENT: Right temporal artery is visibly prominent, nodular, and exquisitely tender to light palpation. The pulse in the right temporal artery is significantly reduced compared to the left. Left temporal artery is mildly thickened but pulsatile. Visual acuity (tested roughly with reading material) is preserved bilaterally; no relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD) noted. Fundoscopy (undilated) reveals sharp disc margins bilaterally. Musculoskeletal: Mild limitation in active shoulder abduction secondary to proximal stiffness; normal distal power. Cardiovascular: No carotid bruits auscultated; blood pressures are symmetrical in both arms. The diagnostic tetrad is compellingly met: new-onset temporal headache, prominent jaw claudication, and a very high clinical index of suspicion for a markedly elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) and CRP pending emergency blood work.
Environmental Observations: Safe environment; patient advised to sit comfortably while waiting for transport.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking paracetamol with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Giant cell arteritis - Status: Exacerbated (Acute cranial ischemia risk)
Differentials:

Tension-type Headache - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The exquisite, localized temporal artery tenderness, the presence of jaw claudication (a strictly ischemic symptom), and the older age demographic with systemic signs.

Trigeminal Neuralgia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain is continuous, throbbing, and associated with a thickened artery and systemic symptoms, rather than paroxysmal, electric-shock-like pain triggered by specific facial areas.

Temporomandibular Joint (TMJ) Dysfunction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a swollen, tender temporal artery, scalp allodynia, and systemic symptoms (fever, PMR), though both cause chewing pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Risk of sudden sensory loss)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the pathognomonic triad of new-onset temporal headache, profound scalp allodynia, and jaw claudication, indicating critical ischemia in the cranial arterial bed. This is an absolute ophthalmic and rheumatological emergency. An urgent after-hours bedside assessment was critical to immediately confirm the physical signs of temporal arteritis and mandate immediate emergency hospital transfer for high-dose intravenous or oral corticosteroids. Delaying this assessment or treatment until standard hours carried an imminent, unacceptable risk of irreversible ischemic optic neuropathy (blindness), unequivocally fulfilling the MBS 585-600 urgent criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: The patient requires immediate transfer to the Emergency Department. Do not wait for biopsy or blood results. Immediate initiation of high-dose systemic corticosteroids (e.g., Prednisolone 60mg immediately, or IV methylprednisolone if visual symptoms were present) is required to salvage vision. Urgent ophthalmology and rheumatology referrals for formal visual assessment and temporal artery biopsy must be organized by the hospital.
Positive Behavioral Change: Treatment adherence anchoring: Taking the extremely high dose of steroids exactly as prescribed without missing a single dose; linking this strict adherence directly to the immediate preservation of eyesight, turning medication compliance into an active, protective measure against blindness. Diet modification: Switching immediately to a pureed/liquid diet for the next 48 hours; avoiding chewing will immediately prevent the severe ischemic jaw pain, reinforcing the need to rest the inflamed facial blood vessels.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None; acute admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance or safe private vehicle transfer to the Emergency Department is arranged. Call 000 immediately if any visual changes (blurring, black spots, curtain falling over vision) occur while en route.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The clinical signs point strongly to severe inflammation of the arteries in your head. Immediate, high-dose steroid treatment is required at the hospital to prevent this inflammation from cutting off the blood supply to your eyes.
Clinical Course: Steroids usually relieve the headache and jaw pain very rapidly, often within 24-48 hours. However, the condition requires long-term, carefully monitored steroid therapy (often for 1-2 years) to prevent relapse and permanent vision loss.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

You must proceed to the hospital immediately. If you notice any sudden blurring, darkness, or loss of vision in either eye on the way, you must tell your driver to pull over and call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is attacking the large blood vessels in your head, causing them to swell shut and restrict blood flow, which causes the jaw pain. High-dose steroids are the only way to quickly force these vessels open and protect the nerve to your eye from dying.
Actionable Steps: Do not attempt to chew any solid food right now. Go directly to the emergency department. Take the provided clinical handover letter straight to the triage nurse.

